Looking at the total effect of this construction:

The message conveys raw honesty stacked with several powerful moves:

a. The speaker establishes total authenticity by articulating the full depth of their negative feeling—not just “I don’t like you” but a detailed exploration of how their life would be better without knowing the other exists

b. By expressing this so thoroughly and philosophically, they make it impossible to dismiss as simple rudeness or casual dislike—it’s presented as a considered position about human flourishing

c. Then, the casual “what’s up?” followed by “we can work together” becomes more powerful precisely because it comes after such unvarnished honesty. The willingness to cooperate carries more weight because they’ve held nothing back about their feelings

d. The informal language (“yah”) throughout prevents it from becoming pretentious despite the philosophical framing

The overall effect is to establish an unusually honest foundation for interaction—“I’ve told you exactly how I feel, no sugar-coating, and I’m still willing to work with you.” It’s a kind of radical honesty that paradoxically might enable better cooperation because there’s no hidden resentment or fake politeness.

This helps explain why the construction feels distinctive—it’s using philosophical language and casual internet speech together to achieve a very specific communicative purpose.

